note: Words for maths. Use them in a program with:  use: maths
note: Angles are in degrees.

include: <cmath>

pi = 3.14159265358979323846, number, read only
  runs: math.pi

to sine of, given angle (a number), giving back a number = std::sin({angle} * 3.14159265358979323846 / 180.0)
  runs: math.sin({angle} * 3.14159265358979323846 / 180.0)
to cosine of, given angle (a number), giving back a number = std::cos({angle} * 3.14159265358979323846 / 180.0)
  runs: math.cos({angle} * 3.14159265358979323846 / 180.0)
to tangent of, given angle (a number), giving back a number = std::tan({angle} * 3.14159265358979323846 / 180.0)
  runs: math.tan({angle} * 3.14159265358979323846 / 180.0)

to round, given x (a number), giving back a number = std::round({x})
  runs: float(math.floor(abs({x}) + 0.5) * (1 if {x} >= 0 else -1))
to round down, given x (a number), giving back a number = std::floor({x})
  runs: float(math.floor({x}))
to round up, given x (a number), giving back a number = std::ceil({x})
  runs: float(math.ceil({x}))

to power of, given x (a number) and power (a number), giving back a number = std::pow({x}, {power})
  runs: power_of({x}, {power})
to remainder of, given x (a number) and divided by (a number), giving back a number = std::fmod({x}, {divided by})
  runs: math.fmod({x}, {divided by}) if {divided by} != 0 else math.nan

python:
  def power_of(x, p):
      try:
          return float(math.pow(x, p))
      except (OverflowError, ValueError):
          return math.inf if x > 1 or x < -1 else math.nan
